From: Raul Moran <rmg@raulmoranguerra.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 09:10:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] SlaveTimeSource: Optionally run on the master's thread

A slave time source hands every grant from its master to its sinks on a
dispatcher thread of its own: per quantum, the master wakes the slave's
thread, the slave's thread wakes its sinks' threads, and the reports come
back the same way. With N machines on slave time sources
(`new Machine(createLocalTimeSource: true)`), that is two thread
hand-offs a quantum per machine and 2N + 1 threads that spin while they
wait.

`RunsOnMasterThread`, set before the slave is attached to its master,
removes the slave's thread. When the master grants the slave time (or
unblocks it), it begins the slave's iteration on its own thread
(`InnerExecuteBegin`: the slave requests the interval with `noWait` and
grants it to its sinks), and when it waits for the slave it ends it
(`InnerExecuteEnd`: the slave waits for its sinks, runs its sync phase and
reports back). The slave's sinks still run on their own threads, beside
the master's other sinks; only the hop through the slave's thread is gone.
The slave's sync phase runs on the master's thread with the slave's time
stamp (`TimeDomainsManager.SwapCurrentThread`). A grant longer than the
slave's quantum is run quantum by quantum in the second half, as the
dispatcher does.

Off by default; when off, nothing changes. The slave time source tests
run both ways.
